# ---------- Lesson B: how atoms combine ----------
B('How atoms combine: molecules, covalent and ionic bonds','Molecules, covalent bonds and ionic bonds','Why atoms hold hands',
 "Think of friends sharing a notebook so that both can study, and of a shopkeeper giving a coin to a customer in a fair exchange. Atoms do something like this too. Some share electrons, some hand them over, and in both cases they end up more stable than before.",
 [("A molecule is an electrically neutral group of more than one atom that can exist on its own and shows all the properties of that substance. A hydrogen molecule has two hydrogen atoms, and a hydrogen chloride molecule has one hydrogen and one chlorine atom.","molecule"),
  ("Some elements, such as helium, exist only as single atoms because their atoms are already stable.","helium: single atoms"),
  ("An atom is stable with 8 electrons in its valence shell, or 2 if the K shell is the outermost. Atoms with fewer electrons share, gain or lose electrons to reach this.","octet and duplet"),
  ("When atoms combine, the energy of the system falls below the total energy of the separate atoms, so the arrangement is more stable. The force that holds the atoms together is a chemical bond.","chemical bond"),
  ("In a covalent bond atoms share electrons. Hydrogen has one electron and needs one more to fill its K shell, so two hydrogen atoms share one pair and form H2, written H-H, a single bond.","covalent bond: H-H"),
  ("Chlorine has seven valence electrons and needs one more, so two chlorine atoms share one pair to make Cl2, written Cl-Cl.","Cl-Cl"),
  ("Oxygen has six valence electrons and needs two, so two oxygen atoms share two pairs of electrons. They are joined by a double bond, written O=O.","O=O double bond"),
  ("Atoms of different elements also share. In hydrogen chloride one hydrogen and one chlorine share a pair, H-Cl. In water, oxygen needs two electrons, so it shares one with each of two hydrogen atoms, giving H2O.","HCl and H2O"),
  ("Covalent compounds are named using prefixes: mono, di, tri, tetra, penta, hexa. CO is carbon monoxide, CO2 is carbon dioxide, SF6 is sulfur hexafluoride, and N2O5 is dinitrogen pentoxide. The first element gets no mono, and hydrogen as first element gets no prefix, so H2S is hydrogen sulfide.","naming covalent compounds"),
  ("In an ionic bond, electrons are transferred. A sodium atom (11 protons) loses its one valence electron and becomes a sodium cation, Na+, with 11 protons and 10 electrons.","Na -> Na+ + e-"),
  ("A chlorine atom gains that electron and becomes a chloride anion, Cl-. The opposite charges attract strongly, and this electrostatic attraction between ions is the ionic bond in sodium chloride.","Cl + e- -> Cl-, NaCl"),
  ("In a crystal of sodium chloride each Na+ is surrounded by six Cl- and each Cl- by six Na+, in a repeating three-dimensional pattern called a crystal lattice. So ionic compounds form crystals, not separate molecules.","crystal lattice"),
  ("Metals usually form cations and non-metals form anions. Sulfur gains two electrons to become S2-, and magnesium loses two to become Mg2+. Ionic compounds are named cation first, then the anion with the ending -ide, for example sodium chloride and calcium oxide.","cation, anion, -ide"),
  ("Ions with more than one kind of atom, such as OH-, NO3-, CO3 2-, SO4 2- and NH4+, are polyatomic ions. Their names do not usually end in -ide.","polyatomic ions")],
 ("Show how magnesium chloride is formed from magnesium (2, 8, 2) and chlorine (2, 8, 7).",
  [("Magnesium has 2 valence electrons. Losing both gives the stable arrangement 2, 8, so it forms Mg2+.","Metals with fewer than four valence electrons tend to lose them."),
   ("Chlorine has 7 valence electrons and needs only 1 more to reach 8, so it forms Cl-.","Non-metals with more than four valence electrons tend to gain."),
   ("One magnesium atom gives 2 electrons, but each chlorine atom accepts only 1, so two chlorine atoms are needed.","The electrons given must equal the electrons taken."),
   ("One Mg2+ ion and two Cl- ions are held together by electrostatic attraction. This is an ionic bond and the compound is magnesium chloride, MgCl2.","Charges balance: 2+ and 2 x 1-.")]),
 ("Compare how a covalent bond and an ionic bond form, using one example of each.",
  ["Covalent bond: atoms share electrons, for example hydrogen chloride H-Cl","Ionic bond: one atom transfers electrons to another to form oppositely charged ions that attract, for example sodium chloride","Both help atoms reach a stable octet or duplet"],
  ["Thinking ionic compounds form separate molecules","Thinking the shared electrons in a covalent bond belong to only one atom"]),
 ["A molecule is a neutral group of atoms that can exist independently and shows the properties of the substance.","A covalent bond forms by sharing electrons; an ionic bond forms by transfer of electrons and the attraction between ions.","Metals tend to form cations and non-metals anions; ionic compounds form crystals, not molecules.","Covalent compounds are named with prefixes; ionic compounds with cation first and anion ending in -ide."],
 [mcq("b-1",1,"What type of bond holds the atoms in a hydrogen chloride (HCl) molecule?","a covalent bond",[("an ionic bond","HCl forms by sharing a pair of electrons, not by transfer."),("a metallic bond","This chapter deals with covalent and ionic bonds.")],"One hydrogen and one chlorine atom share one pair of electrons."),
  mcq("b-2",1,"How many pairs of electrons do two oxygen atoms share in an O2 molecule?","two",[("one","One shared pair makes a single bond, as in H2 and Cl2."),("three","Three pairs would be a triple bond, as in nitrogen.")],"Each oxygen needs two electrons, so they share two pairs: O=O."),
  tf("b-3",1,"Helium exists as single atoms because its atoms are already stable.",True,"Its valence shell is already full, so it does not need to combine."),
  tf("b-4",1,"A sodium atom becomes a Na+ ion by gaining one electron.",False,"It loses one valence electron and becomes positively charged."),
  num("b-5",2,"How many electrons does a Na+ ion have? (Atomic number of sodium = 11)",10,"11 protons and 10 electrons gives a charge of 1+.",[(11,"That is the number of protons."),(12,"Sodium has no reason to have more electrons than protons here.")]),
  mcq("b-6",2,"What is the correct name of CO?","carbon monoxide",[("monocarbon monoxide","The first element does not get the prefix mono."),("carbon monooxide","The final o of mono is dropped before oxide: monoxide.")],"The first element keeps its name; mono is used only on the second element."),
  mcq("b-7",2,"What is the correct name of H2S?","hydrogen sulfide",[("dihydrogen sulfide","No prefix is used when hydrogen is the first element."),("hydrogen disulfide","There is only one sulfur atom.")],"H2S is hydrogen sulfide."),
  mcq("b-8",3,"Which ion does sulfur form?","S2-",[("S2+","Sulfur has six valence electrons, so it gains electrons and becomes negative."),("S6-","It needs only two more electrons to complete its octet.")],"Sulfur gains two electrons to become S2-."),
  mcq("b-9",3,"In sodium chloride, what holds Na+ and Cl- together?","electrostatic attraction between opposite charges",[("sharing of a pair of electrons","Sharing is covalent bonding."),("repulsion between the ions","Like charges repel; opposite charges attract.")],"This attraction is the ionic bond."),
  num("b-10",3,"How many valence electrons does a chlorine atom (2, 8, 7) have?",7,"The outermost shell holds 7 electrons.",[(8,"Chlorine needs 8 to be stable but has only 7."),(17,"That is the atomic number.")])])

# ---------- Lesson C: formulae, properties, masses ----------
B('Chemical formulae, properties of compounds and masses','Writing formulae, properties of ionic and covalent compounds, molecular mass','A quick way to write formulae',
 "A shopkeeper needs to know how many screws match how many nuts. A chemist needs to know how many atoms or ions of each kind make a compound. The criss-cross method gives this quickly, and the same ideas help us understand why salt conducts electricity in water but sugar does not.",
 [("To write the formula of a covalent compound, write the symbols, write the valencies, and criss-cross the valencies as subscripts. Hydrogen (1) and chlorine (1) give HCl; hydrogen (1) and sulfur (2) give H2S; carbon (4) and chlorine (1) give CCl4. A subscript of 1 is not written.","criss-cross the valencies"),
  ("For an ionic compound, write the cation first and then the anion, with the charges below the symbols, and criss-cross only the numbers. Calcium 2+ and chloride 1- give CaCl2; aluminium 3+ and oxide 2- give Al2O3.","cation first, then anion"),
  ("Then divide the subscripts by any common factor. Magnesium 2+ and oxide 2- give Mg2O2, which is simplified to MgO. Calcium 2+ and carbonate 2- give CaCO3.","simplest ratio"),
  ("When two or more of the same polyatomic ion are needed, put it in brackets. Magnesium hydroxide is Mg(OH)2, aluminium hydroxide is Al(OH)3, and aluminium sulfate is Al2(SO4)3. Writing AlOH3 is wrong.","brackets for polyatomic ions"),
  ("Now the properties. In Activity 9.4 you test camphor, sodium chloride, copper sulfate, sugar and naphthalene for solubility in water, kerosene and petrol, and for conduction of electricity. Use low-voltage batteries and keep petrol and kerosene away from flames.","Activity 9.4: safety first"),
  ("Ionic compounds like sodium chloride and copper sulfate generally dissolve in water but not in kerosene or petrol. Covalent compounds like camphor and naphthalene are usually insoluble in water but dissolve in kerosene and petrol.","ionic: water; covalent: kerosene, petrol"),
  ("Ionic solids do not conduct because their ions are fixed in position. When dissolved in water the ions are free to move and carry current, so the bulb glows. Sugar dissolves in water, but it is covalent and gives no ions, so it does not conduct.","ions must be free to move"),
  ("Ionic compounds generally have high melting and boiling points because of strong attraction between ions, whereas covalent compounds usually have low ones.","ionic: high m.p.; covalent: low m.p."),
  ("Molecular mass of a covalent compound is the sum of the atomic masses of all its atoms. Water: H2O = 2 x 1 + 16 = 18 u. Carbon dioxide: 12 + 2 x 16 = 44 u.","molecular mass: H2O = 18 u, CO2 = 44 u"),
  ("Ionic compounds do not form molecules, so we use the formula unit, the simplest whole-number ratio of ions, and its formula unit mass. Sodium oxide Na2O = 2 x 23 + 16 = 62 u. Calcium nitrate Ca(NO3)2 = 40 + 2 x (14 + 48) = 164 u.","formula unit mass")],
 ("Write the formula of aluminium sulfate and find its formula unit mass. (Al = 27 u, S = 32 u, O = 16 u)",
  [("Aluminium ion is Al3+ and sulfate ion is SO4 2-.","Write the cation first, then the anion."),
   ("Criss-cross the numbers 3 and 2: Al gets 2 and SO4 gets 3.","Only the numbers are criss-crossed."),
   ("SO4 appears three times, so it goes in brackets: Al2(SO4)3.","Brackets are needed for more than one polyatomic ion."),
   ("Formula unit mass = 2 x 27 + 3 x (32 + 4 x 16) = 54 + 3 x 96 = 54 + 288 = 342 u.","Multiply each atomic mass by its number of atoms and add.")]),
 ("A solid compound does not conduct electricity but its water solution does. What kind of bond does it have, and why does it conduct only when dissolved?",
  ["It has ionic bonds","In the solid the ions are fixed in position","In solution the ions are free to move and carry current"],
  ["Thinking all compounds that dissolve in water conduct","Thinking solid ionic compounds conduct because they contain charges"]),
 ["Formulae come from criss-crossing valencies or charges; divide by common factors and use brackets for repeated polyatomic ions.","Ionic compounds: soluble in water, conduct when dissolved or molten, high melting points. Covalent compounds: usually soluble in kerosene or petrol and low melting points.","Molecular mass is the sum of atomic masses in a molecule; formula unit mass is used for ionic compounds."],
 [mcq("c-1",1,"What is the formula of calcium chloride?","CaCl2",[("Ca2Cl","The charges 2+ and 1- criss-cross to CaCl2."),("CaCl","The charges do not balance in CaCl.")],"Ca2+ and Cl- criss-cross to CaCl2."),
  mcq("c-2",1,"What is the formula of aluminium oxide?","Al2O3",[("AlO","The charges 3+ and 2- do not balance."),("Al3O2","The numbers are criss-crossed the wrong way.")],"Al3+ and O2- criss-cross to Al2O3."),
  mcq("c-3",1,"Which is the correct formula of aluminium hydroxide?","Al(OH)3",[("AlOH3","Brackets are needed to show three hydroxide ions."),("Al3(OH)","The numbers are criss-crossed the wrong way.")],"Three OH- ions go with one Al3+."),
  num("c-4",2,"Find the molecular mass (u) of methane, CH4. (C = 12 u, H = 1 u)",16,"12 + 4 x 1 = 16 u.",[(13,"There are four hydrogen atoms, not one."),(28,"Hydrogen is 1 u, not 4 u, per atom.")],"u"),
  num("c-5",2,"Find the molecular mass (u) of nitric acid, HNO3. (H = 1, N = 14, O = 16)",63,"1 + 14 + 3 x 16 = 63 u.",[(31,"There are three oxygen atoms, not one."),(47,"You counted only two oxygen atoms.")],"u"),
  tf("c-6",2,"Solid sodium chloride conducts electricity because it contains ions.",False,"In the solid the ions are fixed in position, so it does not conduct."),
  num("c-7",2,"Find the formula unit mass (u) of magnesium hydroxide, Mg(OH)2. (Mg = 24, O = 16, H = 1)",58,"24 + 2 x (16 + 1) = 58 u.",[(41,"You forgot that the bracket multiplies both O and H by 2.")],"u"),
  mcq("c-8",3,"Which compound dissolves in kerosene but not in water?","camphor",[("sodium chloride","It is ionic and dissolves in water."),("copper sulfate","It is ionic and dissolves in water.")],"Camphor is covalent."),
  short("c-9",3,"Write the formula of magnesium oxide.",["mgo"],"Mg2+ and O2- criss-cross to Mg2O2, simplified to MgO.",[("mg2o2","The subscripts must be divided by their common factor.")]),
  mcq("c-10",3,"Which is the formula of potassium carbonate, from K+ and CO3 2-?","K2CO3",[("KCO3","Two K+ are needed to balance one CO3 2-."),("K(CO3)2","Only one carbonate ion is needed.")],"Two K+ balance one CO3 2-.")])
